WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.640
Today, we're going to be speaking to William
Irvine, who's a professor of philosophy at

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.310
Wright State University and author of many
books, including the one I most recently read,

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:14.730
A Guide to the Good Life, The Ancient Art
of Stoic Joy.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.780
So great to have you on.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.419
I really appreciate your time.

00:00:17.419 --> 00:00:19.289
Oh, my pleasure to be here.

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:25.230
So I think I think that this stoicism is a
particularly relevant topic right now for

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.710
all those following American politics and
thinking about the next few weeks.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:36.710
But before we we so obviously link these ideas
to what's going on.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:42.490
Maybe we can speak a little generally about
the ideas of stoicism and some of the most

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:47.850
notable purveyors of these ideas that you
write about, including Seneca and Marcus Aurelius

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.560
and and others.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:57.690
Maybe first you can disabuse some of our audience
about what Stoicism is not, which is maybe

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.990
an indifference to what is going on in the
world, an indifference to tragedy and indifference

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.429
to the struggles of life.

00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:06.429
That's not what it is.

00:01:06.429 --> 00:01:07.899
As you write about in the book.

00:01:07.899 --> 00:01:11.810
What's the right thought process for thinking
about stoicism?

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:17.259
Yeah, the strange thing is the Stoics turn
out to have been cheerful individuals and

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:19.340
you wouldn't think that.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:25.799
So I like to distinguish between lower case
as Stoics and these are people who are simply

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:28.420
gloomy and glum.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.360
And they just their idea of what you do in
life is you just stand there and withstand

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.439
whatever life throws at you.

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:39.399
And that's not the Stoics uppercase as Stoics
at all.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:40.619
And these were individuals.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:45.249
You mentioned some of the names, people like
Seneca, people like like Epictetus, people

00:01:45.249 --> 00:01:50.360
like Marcus Aurelius, who on realized life
is tough.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:56.139
And so you have to have a strategy for dealing
with a life that, you know is going to be

00:01:56.139 --> 00:01:58.119
tough in there.

00:01:58.119 --> 00:01:59.950
They were not anti emotion.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.009
They were anti negative emotions.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:06.509
And they thought quite sensibly that what
you should do in your life is you should try

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:10.240
to minimize the number of negative emotions
you experience.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.380
And these are emotions like anger, anxiety,
envy and grief.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.420
But they had absolutely nothing against positive
emotions.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.610
And in fact, they thought we should be embracing
positive emotions.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.310
Positive emotions are different than pleasures.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.310
Okay.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.150
So it's a important distinction, but positive
emotions like feelings of delight and even

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.200
feelings of joy.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.830
That's what we should be in pursuit of.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:44.290
And these guys were the preeminent psychologists
of their time, and they hit upon some psychological

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.840
strategies that work wonderfully well.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.810
And so what I've done in my books is I've
tried to share those strategies with a broader

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.310
public.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.910
So I think that this will help.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.370
A lot of this is just the timing of this conversation.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:07.350
It is beautifully timed because I think many
of the things you talk about in the book,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:13.010
including negative visualization and understanding
the dichotomy, are really the tri Kotomi of

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.410
control.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:21.600
These things are so relevant for anybody who
is worried about the election results that

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.140
we are waiting for.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:28.940
Can you talk a little bit about maybe to start
with the idea of negative visualization and

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:32.480
its sort of relevance to day to day life?

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.480
Yeah.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.640
Negative visualizations as simple technique.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.360
And what you do is you pause in your daily
business.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.080
It only takes a few seconds to do.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.830
And you think about something that's important
to you, something that plays a very significant

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:47.890
role in your life.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.680
And it could be your job.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.760
It could be your spouse or your significant
other.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.170
It could be your children.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:04:01.350
And now take a moment to imagine that that
thing disappeared from your life.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.660
And there are lots of ways that can harm,
you know, people.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:10.710
Many people have recently lost a job or split
up with their significant others.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.890
Imagine that happening.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:17.810
And now the point is you don't dwell on that
because that would be a recipe for a miserable

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:18.989
existence.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:24.290
What you do instead is you allow yourself
to have a few seconds in which you really

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.060
think about that, then go on with your daily
business.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:33.570
And it's almost a magical effect, because
next time you encounter the person that you

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:40.540
imagined losing or you imagine being separated
from, it'll be a different experience because

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.670
you will no longer be taking that person for
granted.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.830
And that's the problem with human existence.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:52.570
You know, whatever we're whatever we we have
and worked so hard to get, once we've got

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.400
it, we take it for granted.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.220
And we think, oh, well, this is fine.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:03.360
But, you know, if only I had some more, you
know, a different, significant other a different

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.590
job, I would finally be happy.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.639
So we put ourselves on this treadmill.

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.169
And what a sad way to spend the one life that
you have to live.

00:05:12.169 --> 00:05:13.169
Yes.

00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:19.890
Sometimes the concept of He Doneck adaptation
is used for this sort of way in which we as

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:28.139
humans very quickly establish the status quo
as a new baseline, even if it's better, for

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:29.669
example, or it could be worse.

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:35.290
But even if it's better than 99 percent of
our prior existence on the planet, it very

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:41.330
quickly becomes the new baseline from which
our existence is sort of judged.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.580
Am I wrong to think that the idea of negative
visualization may be a sort of antidote to

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.590
that adaptation that takes place?

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.060
Yeah, it's it's like it's like an ointment.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:55.919
You know, when you start taking your life
for for granted, it's anointment that you

00:05:55.919 --> 00:05:57.680
can apply as needed.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.449
And and it is interesting, you know, Stoics
and they aren't alone in doing this, but a

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:08.460
lot of the philosophies of the ancient world
advised you to not only be a spectator of

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.580
the lives of other people, which is what we
kind of instinctively do.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.790
But be a spectator of your own life.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:17.570
Watch yourself.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:19.080
Watch what motivates you.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.610
Watch the way you react to the events of the
day.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.180
And then really think about what you're doing,
because, you know, you can look around you

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:33.060
and you can see people who work so hard to
get something and they finally get it.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:39.230
And they're really, really happy for an hour,
a day or maybe a week.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:43.430
And then the new wears off and they're right
back where they were before.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:49.100
You know, it's it's like a poor a poor, poor
guy chasing mirages across the desert.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.490
You know, off there in the distance there's
water.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:57.080
And if only I can crawl to it, I will at last
have water to drink.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.870
Gets there and it it evaporates.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:06:59.870
It's gone.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:00.870
Right.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.580
And then just has to do that again.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.639
You've got, like I say, well, life to live.

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:10.479
And, you know, what we do is we look around,
we see how other people are living their lives

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:12.790
and we assume somebody has done their homework.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:15.470
Somebody somewhere has figured this out.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.430
So I'll just copy them.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:21.830
But throughout the millennia, there have been
people who have stood back and looked at this

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.500
and said, you know, nobody's done their homework.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.250
And by by doing what?

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:31.130
But other people are doing in this case, you're
only going to get yourself in trouble at some

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:35.260
kind of superficial level that this might
sound very anti sort of consumerist.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:40.270
The idea that there's no product one can buy
that is going to sort of give them whatever

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:45.229
degree of happiness they might imagine they
would achieve by by buying a certain product.

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:53.080
But when I read the book, I got more the impression
that it's not necessarily that, but it's more

00:07:53.080 --> 00:08:02.449
about making decisions consciously and thoughtfully
and and not just writing off where we get

00:08:02.449 --> 00:08:04.400
and maintaining that appreciation.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.680
Can you comment about that a little bit?

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:11.380
I mean, this is it doesn't sound necessarily
like an anticapitalist or anti consumer culture

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:17.000
ideology, but it's more about recognizing
and understanding how we are sort of wired,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.070
in a sense, to very quickly adjust to whatever
it is that we have.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.180
Yeah.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:26.390
You know, if you if you believe in evolution,
I and I do.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.930
Our ancestors back in Africa who were perfectly
happy with what they had.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.300
They didn't survive.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.279
They didn't spread their their genes.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:38.260
The ones that survived were the ones that
were constantly thinking about things that

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.120
had happened, trying to learn from them.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.380
So they didn't make the mistakes over.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.010
Kind to think about what was going to happen.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:49.860
You know, this idea, this this dream of living
in the present moment, our ancestors on the

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:54.440
savannas of Africa who did that got eaten
by a lion, you know, and that was that was

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.120
the end of of their story.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:03.510
So there is this thing that I refer to as
the Gap Theory of happiness, and that is that

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:07.149
there is a gap between what you have and what
you want.

00:09:07.149 --> 00:09:08.149
And then.

00:09:08.149 --> 00:09:13.330
So one way to close that gap and gain happiness
is by working hard to get the thing that you

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:14.330
want.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.950
But there's another way, and that is reverse
the gap the other way.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:24.140
You learn to want the things you already have
because and there there will be a lot of people

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.940
who who reject this claim on the face of it.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:33.810
You have so much in your life and whatever
it is you think you absolutely need to have,

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:38.700
look at your life and look at how your ancestors
would have looked at your life.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.560
They would have regarded you as dreaming,
as living in a dream world.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.390
You have all of this stuff.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:45.390
What?

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:49.680
You have a device in your pocket that cannot
only talk to somebody in China, but you can

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.740
see them talking while you do it.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.500
That's just crazy.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.760
And yet here, people.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.690
And people now, you know, when they don't
have the latest model cell phone, they feel,

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:04.100
well, you know, my life is ruined.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.410
So take what you already have.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:07.640
Learn to appreciate it.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:12.270
Learn to be thankful for the existence you
find yourself living.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:15.649
Learn to embrace your life.

00:10:15.649 --> 00:10:20.050
And now if you look back at the at the Stoics.

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:24.230
They didn't simply go into dormant mode where
they said, OK, I'm fine with that.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:30.000
They they turned out to be some very productive
and ambitious people.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:37.200
Seneca, for instance, besides being a philosopher,
was an adviser to an emperor, was the first

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:44.279
century A.D. equivalent of a billionaire and
was one of the greatest playwrights of his

00:10:44.279 --> 00:10:45.279
time.

00:10:45.279 --> 00:10:46.279
So he was no slouch.

00:10:46.279 --> 00:10:52.371
But, you know, the idea of waking up in the
morning and just feeling wonderful that you

00:10:52.371 --> 00:10:55.370
get to be having the life that you're having.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:59.890
And a large part of that is your own attitude
and your own attitude can be is very much

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:01.520
in your control.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.350
So let's that's a perfect Segway.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:11.370
So the one of the things that I see with my
audience that is causing significant anxiety

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:12.790
as we get closer to this election.

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:18.640
And my audience, being a left leaning audience,
is overwhelmingly folks who want to see Donald

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:22.540
Trump go is what have I done enough?

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:25.600
I have anxiety about the results.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.870
And if we introduce the idea of a dichotomy
of control where we would separate things

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:36.060
into that which we can control than that which
we cannot control, it sort of falls short

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:40.980
when it comes to something like an election,
which is where the idea of the Tricon Ayumi

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:46.150
of control is is so useful, those things over
which we have some control.

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:51.660
And I think in the book you talk about a tennis
match where ultimately you don't really have

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.250
unilateral control of the results.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:59.540
But certainly you can have control over your
practice plan.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:04.730
You can have control over your attitude and
expectations going into the match, et cetera.

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:09.340
And for me, that's been a perfect analogy
to the election where, well, we're all voters.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:13.649
And as voters, we do have some control and
we control our one vote.

00:12:13.649 --> 00:12:18.339
We also can decide whether to phone bank or
not and if so, in which states.

00:12:18.339 --> 00:12:22.600
But ultimately with one hundred and thirty
or one hundred and thirty five million expected

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.650
votes, no one of us can control the results.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:26.650
So can you.

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:32.420
Can you give us a sense of a healthy way to
sort of apply this to something like a national

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:38.399
election with a not infinite but very large
number of moving parts, many of which over

00:12:38.399 --> 00:12:39.910
we don't have control?

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:44.020
Yeah, this is the most complicated election
of my lifetime.

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:48.089
So much going on, so much unpredictability.

00:12:48.089 --> 00:12:49.770
And I'm ready to be surprised.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:51.580
That's my plan going into it.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:58.800
And, you know, the last election in 2016,
I'm an extreme early riser.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:02.980
And I was up at about 2:00 in the morning
and I turned on the TV expecting to see Hillary's

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.110
face there beaming.

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.019
And it was Donald Trump instead.

00:13:07.019 --> 00:13:08.470
And I thought, wow.

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:11.090
So I'm ready for for absolutely anything.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.680
The dichotomy of control.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:14.950
There are things you can control and things
you can't.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:18.220
And if you spend your time worrying about
things you can't control, you're going to

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:22.210
be a very anxious and unhappy individual.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:28.269
So think about things you can control and
do those to the best of your ability.

00:13:28.269 --> 00:13:31.860
So in the case of a tennis match, you can
control how hard you train.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.519
You can control whether you party the night
before her, whether you tried to get a good

00:13:35.519 --> 00:13:36.550
night's sleep.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.180
You can control your strategy.

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:42.140
Can you control how hard the other person
practices?

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:43.970
No, you can't.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:47.529
So that does that isn't even part of your
equation in.

00:13:47.529 --> 00:13:50.970
By the way, we come into the notion here of
success and failure.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.180
So for a stoic what counts as success?

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.430
Success is to put it into a motto.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:02.660
Success is doing what you could with what
you had, where you were.

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:08.440
If you did those things, you have succeeded
because what else could you do?

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:09.440
Right.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:14.089
You did what you could with the election.

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:16.279
Stoics would have a two stage plan.

00:14:16.279 --> 00:14:19.440
Number one is don't worry about things you
have no control over.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:25.140
And number two is be emotionally prepared
for surprises.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:30.589
So the Stoics were were big on what I call
stoic training, where you go out of your way

00:14:30.589 --> 00:14:34.950
to do things that are difficult just because
they're hard to do, do things where there's

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:39.920
a significant chance of failure, not catastrophic
failure, but failure in some sense of the

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:40.920
word.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:41.920
How come?

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.000
Just so you can get used to failing.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:48.910
You know, successful people are typically
people who have experienced a lot of failure.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:51.460
What makes them special is when they failed.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:52.470
They didn't break.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:53.610
They bounced.

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:54.890
They took the failure.

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:56.860
They learned from the failure.

00:14:56.860 --> 00:15:01.810
They went on to do new, better failures than
they had before.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.800
And finally popped out at the end of the tunnel.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:08.339
What's conventionally called a successful
person.

00:15:08.339 --> 00:15:10.089
So with this.

00:15:10.089 --> 00:15:15.800
Prepare yourself for just about anything emotionally
and just say don't know where it's going.

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:21.881
But wherever it it goes, what I will do is
I will do what I can with what I've got, where

00:15:21.881 --> 00:15:26.060
I am.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:16:55.820
We will pause our conversation 

